Package: timeshift-recovery
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         timeshift-ssh | timeshift,
         grub2-common,
         util-linux,
         coreutils
Recommends: mmdebstrap,
            squashfs-tools,
            dpkg-repack,
            parted,
            gdisk,
            e2fsprogs
Suggests: imagemagick
Description: Provision a bootable Timeshift recovery environment
 timeshift-recovery builds a minimal Ubuntu recovery environment on this
 machine, places it on a dedicated partition (or on the root filesystem
 when there is no unallocated space), and adds a GRUB entry that boots it
 when R is pressed at startup.
 .
 The environment exists to solve one problem: Timeshift snapshots stored
 on a remote host over SSH cannot be restored from a system that will not
 boot, because the restore tool, the network stack and the wireless driver
 all live on the broken root filesystem. The recovery environment carries
 its own copy of all three, plus the snapshot location already configured,
 so a restore needs nothing but the machine itself.
 .
 The environment is built with mmdebstrap against this host's own apt
 sources, so it always matches the installed release and architecture, and
 it mirrors the host's linux-firmware packages so the hardware it has to
 rescue is the hardware it supports. A dpkg trigger rebuilds it whenever a
 new Timeshift is installed, so the rescue environment never lags behind
 the system it is meant to rescue.
